{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793372778,"nonce":"FIKD0/1o6qG2VTxGOriKLcgzF3w=","title":"wasm: String.charCodeAt costs O(length) per call","message":"`haxe_string_char_code_at_ptr` in runtime-wasm validated the whole string as UTF-8 and walked `chars().nth(index)` on every call. A loop over a string is quadratic: 100k calls on a 100k-character string took 2.4 s on wasm against under 1 ms native, and the revcomp benchmark (2.5M characters) did not finish in 300 s.\n\nNative caches the character count in the HaxeString header, so a one-byte-per-character string indexes directly. The wasm header is `{ptr, len, cap}` with no room for it; changing that layout touches the backend's data section, every header allocation in runtime-wasm and the host writers in wasm_runner.\n\nFix: memoize the count of strings of 256+ bytes in a side table keyed by data buffer, evicted by `haxe_string_free`; shorter strings are counted per call. After it, the probe runs in under 1 ms and revcomp matches native in 0.8 s.","files":null}]}